<article dtd-version="1.3" article-type="meeting-abstract" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="hwp">ascomtg</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">ascomtg</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">ASCOMTG</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Journal of Clinical Oncology</journal-title><abbrev-journal-title>ASCO MEETING ABSTRACTS</abbrev-journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0732-183X</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1527-7755</issn><publisher><publisher-name>American Society of Clinical Oncology</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="meeting-id">335</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="session-id">17304</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="session-participation-id">8000800</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="temporary-abstract-id">58882</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="abstract-id">rw58882</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/s41389-018-0078-y</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Biochemistry</subject></subj-group><series-title>2026 ASCO Annual Meeting (May 29 - June 2, 2026)</series-title><series-text>Publication Only</series-text></article-categories><title-group><article-title>MEK inhibitors induce apoptosis via FoxO3a-dependent PUMA induction in colorectal cancer cells</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib id="C8000800" contrib-type="presenter"><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>Lin</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8000800" /></contrib><contrib id="C8000801" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ding</surname><given-names>Dapeng</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8000800" /></contrib><contrib id="C8000802" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Jiang</surname><given-names>Yanmei</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8000800" /></contrib><contrib id="C8000803" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>Yan</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8000800" /></contrib><contrib id="C8000804" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>Shijun</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8000800" /></contrib><aff id="A8000800">Department of Clinical Laboratory, the First Affiliated Hospital of Dalian Medical University, Dalian, Liaoning, 116011, China;</aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2018</year></pub-date><fpage>rw58882</fpage><lpage>rw58882</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>ASCO-style conversion for internal testing; source rights remain with the original publisher.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2018</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p><bold>rw58882</bold></p><p>Mutations in BRAF are common to many cancers, including CRC. The MEK inhibitors are being investigated in BRAF -mutant CRC. In this study, we aimed to investigate how MEK inhibitor suppresses growth of BRAF -mutated CRC cells as well as its potential mechanisms. Our findings indicated that MEK inhibitor promote PUMA expression via ERK/FoxO3a signaling pathway. In addition, PUMA induction is essential for MEK inhibitor-induced apoptosis. Moreover, PUMA induction is required for MEK inhibitors to induced apoptosis in combination with cisplatin, dabrafenib, or Gefitinib. Knockdown of PUMA suppressed the anticancer effect of the MEK inhibitor in vivo. Our findings indicate a novel role for PUMA as a regulator of the antitumor effects of MEK inhibitor, suggesting that PUMA induction may modulate MEK inhibitor sensitivity.</p></abstract><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-row-number</meta-name><meta-value>8</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-record-id</meta-name><meta-value>58882.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-journal</meta-name><meta-value>Oncogenesis</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-publisher</meta-name><meta-value>Springer - Nature Publishing Group</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-country</meta-name><meta-value>China</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-article-type</meta-name><meta-value>Research Article;</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>manual-verdict</meta-name><meta-value>UNCLEAR</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-reason</meta-name><meta-value>Euphemisms for Plagiarism;Investigation by Journal/Publisher;Paper Mill;Plagiarism of Data;Unreliable Results and/or Conclusions;</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front></article>
